abstract: >-
  Meisner (1992) built Hadamard matrices, +1/-1 matrices with orthogonal
  rows, of order N = m^2 from one of order m: an m x m array of rank-one
  sign blocks whose order and signs can be chosen freely. We prove three
  results about these matrices and about a second construction, introduced
  here, of order N = 4h^2, in which a transversal design with four groups
  lets each local sign vector serve four blocks. First, a typical output
  reveals which of its rows and columns came from each block, so every
  equivalence must respect the blocks. As a result, all but a fraction o(1)
  of the outputs are rigid: their only signed row and column symmetries are
  the identity and the negation of everything. Second, we count them: for each
  fixed input, up to signed permutations and transposition, the rigid outputs form C classes
  with log C = N log N - cN + O(sqrt(N) log^2 N), where c = 2 - log 2 for
  Meisner's square and c = 2 + (log 2)/8 for four groups. At order 1600, one
  design and one matrix of order 20 give more than 10^1514 rigid regular
  classes. Third, a rank argument shows why such block constructions need
  orders divisible by 16: at orders N = 4 mod 8, every partition of a
  Hadamard matrix into rank-one blocks with no part of size one has all
  parts of size two, as in the Paley II matrices. The constructions give no
  new orders. Novelty is bounded to the sources searched.
keywords:
  - Hadamard matrices
  - rigidity
  - automorphism groups
  - Bush-type Hadamard matrices
  - weaving
  - inequivalent Hadamard matrices
